{
  "dom_id": "step-q344438",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to twelve times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{\u00a0m}}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 12 & =2x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=6\\text{\u00a0m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={6}^{2}=36{\\text{\u00a0m}}^{2}.\n$$",
